"""Prose sections (stage 4 draft) for Paper 2, 'How the 1997–98 El Niño developed'.

build(c, d) returns section dicts. c(*keys) renders numbered citation links; d is a dict of numbers computed from the
reanalysis diagnostics (tools/papers/diag_1997.py) so that no diagnostic value is retyped by hand. Every other number is
taken from the timeline, evidence table or monthly table in make_1997.py.
"""


def build(c, d):
    S = []
    S.append({"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "html": f"""<p>The 1997–98 El Niño peaked at +2.28 on NOAA's Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI) in NDJ 1997 (+2.37 on the traditional ONI) and was documented from its rapid onset to its sudden demise by a newly completed tropical Pacific observing system {c('mcphaden1999')}. We reconstruct its development from those observations, the published event studies and new reanalysis diagnostics, and grade each proposed cause by the strength of event-specific evidence. The equatorial Pacific recharged after the 1995–96 La Niña: warm water volume was positive from December 1995 and peaked in April 1997. Onto that charged state a sequence of westerly wind events from December 1996 to June 1997 — organized by two exceptionally strong Madden–Julian oscillations and amplified by twin cyclones linked to East Asian cold surges — forced downwelling Kelvin waves and pushed the edge of the warm pool east. An ocean-model decomposition attributes about half of the eastern anomaly on 1 June 1997 to that wind stress and about 40% to the initial ocean state. Coupling was established by May–June and the event grew to a December peak. Its end was set by the seasonal southward shift of the westerlies from November 1997 and came abruptly in May 1998. Forecast models predicted warming but none predicted its strength before it was under way. The Indian Ocean Dipole of 1997 followed the Pacific warming and is graded mainly a consequence. We find no basis for attributing the event's amplitude to background warming.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "findings", "title": "Main findings", "html": f"""<ol>
<li><b>Precondition (grade A−; high confidence).</b> Recharge after the 1995–96 La Niña: warm water volume positive from December 1995, +3.12 × 10¹⁴ m³ in April 1997; about 40% of the 1 June 1997 Niño 3 anomaly comes from the initial thermal state in one ocean model {c('cpc', 'meinen2000', 'vanoldenborgh2000')}.</li>
<li><b>Trigger (grade A; high confidence).</b> Westerly wind events in December 1996, March 1997 and April 1997 within a December–June series; wind stress supplies about half the anomaly and is the main difference from 1996, when no El Niño developed {c('vanoldenborgh2000', 'picaut2002', 'lengaigne2002')}.</li>
<li><b>MJO and extratropical amplification of the trigger (grade B; medium–high confidence).</b> Two exceptionally strong MJOs in December 1996 and February 1997 organised the bursts; East Asian cold surges and twin cyclones intensified them {c('bergman2001', 'yurienecker1998', 'mcphadenyu1999')}.</li>
<li><b>Onset mechanism (grade A−; high confidence).</b> Kelvin-wave currents advected warm water east (central Pacific warming in late March – early April 1997), and the March event's own SST response drew in further westerlies {c('bergman2001', 'lengaigne2003', 'boulanger2001')}.</li>
<li><b>Amplifier (grade B; high confidence).</b> Bjerknes feedback from May–June 1997, with zonal advection important in the central Pacific {c('cpc', 'picaut2002', 'wangmcphaden2000')}.</li>
<li><b>Termination (grade A; high confidence).</b> Seasonal southward shift of the westerlies from November 1997, eastward-propagating thermocline shoaling and basin discharge; abrupt surface cooling in May 1998, with an MJO implicated in its timing {c('vecchi2006a', 'vecchi2006b', 'lengaigne2006', 'takayabu1999')}.</li>
<li><b>Indian Ocean (mainly a consequence; grade B).</b> The 1997 positive IOD grew after Pacific coupling, through the reversed Walker circulation; any feedback onto the Pacific rests on model experiments, not tested here for 1997 {c('murtugudde2000', 'yurienecker1999', 'luo2010')}.</li>
<li><b>Predictability.</b> No model forecast even half the peak amplitude at 6–11 months' lead; the rapid succession of bursts in early 1997 was a rare draw from the atmosphere's noise {c('landseaknaff2000', 'barnston1999', 'fedorov2003')}.</li>
</ol>"""})

    S.append({"id": "intro", "title": "1. Introduction", "html": f"""<p>By 1997 the tropical Pacific was far better observed than in 1982. The Tropical Ocean–Global Atmosphere (TOGA) programme had built the TAO array of moored buoys, an island tide-gauge network and satellite missions for surface winds, SST and sea level {c('mcphaden1998tao')}; TOPEX/Poseidon altimetry covered 1992 onward {c('delcroix2000')}; and 1997–98 was the first major event predicted in real time by numerous forecasting groups {c('landseaknaff2000')}. The 1997–98 El Niño nevertheless surprised forecasters in both its speed and its strength, and its abrupt end in May 1998 was also unexpected {c('mcphaden1999', 'picaut2002', 'barnston1999')}.</p>
<p>The event therefore offers a test that 1982–83 could not: whether the processes proposed for El Niño onset — recharge of equatorial heat, wind-forced Kelvin waves, intraseasonal westerly bursts and coupled feedback — can be separated and quantified for a single event when the ocean is observed directly. A lively debate followed over whether the intense westerly wind events or the slower oceanic preconditioning mattered more {c('vanoldenborgh2000')}. This paper reconstructs the sequence month by month and grades each factor, using the same framework, grades and index (RONI) as Paper 1.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "methods", "title": "2. Definitions, data and methods", "html": f"""<p><b>Index.</b> RONI (the three-month mean of relative Niño 3.4, i.e. Niño 3.4 minus the 20°N–20°S tropical-mean anomaly, rescaled) is the primary index {c('noaa2026', 'lheureux2024')}; the traditional ONI is shown alongside. For this event the two agree closely (peak +2.28 vs +2.37), so the classification as a very strong event does not depend on the index choice.</p>
<p><b>Observations.</b> TAO/TRITON moorings provide daily surface winds, SST and thermocline depth along the equator; TOPEX/Poseidon sea level gives Kelvin and Rossby wave signals; NOAA CPC and PMEL indices give monthly SST, SOI, 850 hPa trades, OLR, upper-300 m heat content (180°–100°W, 1981–2010 base) and warm water volume (WWV) {c('cpc', 'mcphaden1998tao', 'meinen2000')}. SST indices are from OISST v2.1 {c('reynolds2007')} and ERSSTv5 {c('huang2017')}.</p>
<p><b>Reanalysis diagnostics.</b> To compare with 1982–83 on identical definitions, the westerly-burst and MJO diagnostics of Paper 1 were re-run for July 1995 – December 1998: daily NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 winds and interpolated OLR, anomalies against a 1979–2000 mean plus three annual harmonics; a burst is a 5°S–5°N 1000 hPa anomaly of at least 5 m s⁻¹ for at least 3 days over at least 10° of longitude within 120°E–150°W. MJO activity is described with the Wheeler–Hendon RMM index and OLR-based measures, whose amplitudes can differ for the same event {c('wheelerhendon2004', 'kiladis2014')}. {d.get('methods_note', '')}</p>
<p><b>Evidence grades.</b> A: event-specific observation plus a model or budget study isolating the effect. B: event-specific observation with a plausible mechanism and supporting studies. C: correlation, composite or general theory only. D: speculative, disputed or untested for this event. The grade describes the link to 1997–98, not the general validity of a mechanism.</p>
<p><b>Limits of this review.</b> For several central studies only the abstract could be read (the reference list records what was read for each); conclusions that depend on details beyond an abstract are marked as such.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "preconditions", "title": "3. Pre-event conditions, mid-1995 – early 1997", "html": f"""<p>The event grew out of the 1995–96 La Niña, which reached RONI −1.02 in OND 1995 {c('cpc')}. As the trades relaxed after that La Niña the equatorial warm-water reservoir refilled: WWV turned positive in December 1995 and stayed positive throughout 1996 (+0.26 to +0.85 × 10¹⁴ m³), while heat content east of the Date Line hovered near zero (−0.47 to +0.17 °C) {c('cpc')}. This is the recharge state that recharge-oscillator theory and the 1980–99 WWV record identify as the precondition for El Niño {c('jin1997', 'meinen2000', 'wyrtki1985b')}.</p>
<p>At the surface, 1996 stayed ENSO-neutral and slightly cool (RONI −0.20 to −0.37 from MAM 1996 to DJF 1997), with the far eastern Pacific cold (Niño 1+2 −0.7 to −1.6 °C) {c('cpc')}. In autumn 1996 the western Pacific was warm and the warm pool extended east, a pattern also present before the aborted event of 1989–90 and before 1982 {c('bergman2001')}. Between November 1996 and January 1997 the eastern Pacific had cold equatorial SST and high sea-level pressure, while off-equatorial warm SST and anomalous cyclones in the far west accompanied a switch of equatorial wind anomalies from easterly to westerly {c('wangweisberg2000')}.</p>
<p>Recharge alone did not determine the outcome. The same ocean-model analysis that credits the initial state with about 40% of the 1 June 1997 Niño 3 anomaly finds that the main difference from 1996 — a year with comparable preconditions but no El Niño — lies in the zonal wind stress {c('vanoldenborgh2000')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "chronology", "title": "4. Development, December 1996 – December 1997", "html": f"""<p><b>Westerly wind events, December 1996 – June 1997.</b> A series of westerly wind bursts from December 1996 to June 1997, notably in March 1997, was instrumental in setting up the event {c('picaut2002')}. The events of December 1996, March 1997 and April 1997 carry most of the wind-stress contribution to the June anomaly {c('vanoldenborgh2000')}. Satellite (SSM/I) winds show the bursts embedded in the active phase of the MJO but greatly enhanced over the western Pacific by equatorial twin cyclones, themselves induced by northerly cold surges from East Asia and the western North Pacific {c('yurienecker1998')}. Two exceptionally strong MJOs, in December 1996 and February 1997, first cooled the western Pacific through cloud shading and evaporation, with off-equatorial upwelling adding to the cooling in February {c('bergman2001')}. {d.get('wwb_sentence', '')}</p>
<p><b>Kelvin waves and the eastward jump of the warm pool.</b> In TAO records from October 1996 to December 1997, intraseasonal variability east of the Date Line was dominated by first-baroclinic Kelvin waves travelling east at 2.4–2.7 m s⁻¹ {c('kutsuwada2002')}, consistent with the 2.4 m s⁻¹ found for the decade before {c('kessler1995')}. The onset was linked to such waves forced by intraseasonal oscillations originating over the Indian Ocean {c('mcphadenyu1999')}. In late March and early April 1997 the Kelvin wave forced by the February MJO warmed the central Pacific, mainly by zonal temperature advection; afterwards winds, currents and SST gradients did not return to their earlier state {c('bergman2001')}. In ocean-model experiments the March event displaced the eastern edge of the warm pool about 2000 km in a month, cooled the far west by about 0.8 °C and warmed the Kelvin-wave path by about 0.5 °C {c('lengaigne2002', 'lengaigne2003')}; a nonlinear interaction with the salinity and temperature fronts at the warm-pool edge tripled the surface currents relative to a linear response {c('boulanger2001')}. In an atmospheric model, the SST changes produced by the March event themselves generated responses that favoured rapid growth {c('lengaigne2003')}.</p>
<p><b>Warming in two places.</b> SST warmed first near the Date Line in January–February 1997 and in the far east from March (Niño 1+2 from −0.45 °C in February to +0.80 °C in April) {c('wangweisberg2000', 'cpc')}. The eastward-moving and westward-spreading warm anomalies merged; once the central and eastern warm regions joined, coupling between SST and winds became fully effective {c('wangweisberg2000', 'picaut2002')}. WWV peaked at +3.12 × 10¹⁴ m³ in April 1997 {c('cpc')}.</p>
<p><b>Coupled growth, May – December 1997.</b> RONI rose from +0.83 (AMJ) to +1.20 (MJJ), with SOI −1.3 and −1.4 and central-Pacific OLR near −26 W m⁻² {c('cpc')}. Growth continued to SON (+2.22) and the peak in NDJ (+2.28); in October the central-Pacific trade index reached −4.1 and OLR −49.8 W m⁻² {c('cpc')}. Much of the western warm water was carried east by anomalous surface currents {c('picaut2002')}. Interannual heat budgets from TAO moorings show zonal advection important everywhere and most significant in the central Pacific, vertical heat flux especially important in the east, and surface heat fluxes generally damping the anomalies {c('wangmcphaden2000')}. Eastern SST anomalies exceeded 4 °C at the peak {c('vecchi2006a')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "termination", "title": "5. Termination and transition, November 1997 – mid-1998", "html": f"""<p>Discharge began before the surface peak. WWV fell to +0.66 × 10¹⁴ m³ in November and turned negative in December 1997 {c('cpc')}. From November, near-Date-Line westerly anomalies shifted south of the equator, a shift reproduced as the atmosphere's deterministic response to the observed SST field; the oceanic response was thermocline shoaling that began in the central Pacific and propagated east {c('vecchi2006b', 'vecchi2006a')}. The same seasonal migration of the warmest water appears as a general mechanism of El Niño termination in observations and coupled models {c('harrisonvecchi1999', 'lengaigne2006')}.</p>
<p>Eastern SST stayed warm from December 1997 to May 1998 despite strong subsurface cooling {c('vecchi2006a')}. Upwelling signals arrived from several directions: Kelvin waves generated by easterlies and boundary reflection from the west, Rossby waves from westerly forcing in the east, and the general uplift of the thermocline as the basin discharged {c('picaut2002', 'mcphadenyu1999', 'delcroix2000')}. In the west, off-equatorial pressure turned high and easterly anomalies developed over the far western Pacific {c('wangweisberg2000')}. When easterly stress returned to the eastern Pacific in May 1998, eastern equatorial SST fell by more than 4 °C within two weeks, and by about 8 °C near 0°, 130°W {c('vecchi2006a', 'vecchi2006b', 'picaut2002')}; the cooling began between 125°W and 170°W {c('mcphadenyu1999')}. Takayabu et al. relate this abrupt termination to an MJO {c('takayabu1999')}. La Niña followed (RONI JJA 1998 −1.05), with the largest equatorial phytoplankton bloom then observed {c('cpc', 'chavez1999')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "interactions", "title": "6. Interactions with the broader climate system", "html": f"""<p><b>Intraseasonal variability and bursts.</b> {d.get('mjo_paragraph', 'Reanalysis diagnostics pending.')} Across many events, MJO activity in the western Pacific in late boreal spring leads El Niño the following autumn {c('hendon2007')}, and stronger MJO Kelvin-wave forcing preceded larger eastern anomalies by 6–12 months in 1980–99 {c('zhanggottschalck2002')}. Yet waveguide warming follows MJO events only when they contain a westerly wind event, and an MJO does not make a burst more likely {c('chiodi2014')}. For 1997 the two meet: the bursts that mattered were embedded in strong MJOs {c('yurienecker1998', 'bergman2001')}. An intermediate coupled model initialised on 1 January 1997 produced a weak warm event on its own, and one about 50% stronger when the rectified effect of MJO-like winds was imposed {c('kesslerkleeman2000')}.</p>
<p><b>Are bursts a cause or a response?</b> Bursts become more likely as the warm pool extends east, and treating them as partly SST-dependent roughly doubles modelled event amplitude {c('eisenman2005', 'gebbie2007')}; statistics also show bursts migrating east ahead of the El Niño peak {c('seikitakayabu2007')}. In 1997 the warm pool had extended east by autumn 1996 {c('bergman2001')}, so some burst activity may already reflect the developing state. This does not remove the trigger role: the decomposition finds the December, March and April events to be the main difference from 1996 {c('vanoldenborgh2000')}, and the impact of a burst depends on its timing relative to the oscillation and on the background state {c('fedorov2002')}.</p>
<p><b>Extratropical influence.</b> The best-documented pathway is synoptic: East Asian cold surges induced the twin cyclones that intensified the bursts {c('yurienecker1998', 'congwen2003')}. The Pacific meridional mode and seasonal footprinting precede many El Niños {c('chang2007', 'vimont2003', 'anderson2003')}, but no study read here isolates them for 1997 (grade C).</p>
<p><b>Decadal background.</b> The PDO was strongly positive through 1997, and the shallow meridional overturning circulation was at a low during 1992–98 before rebounding {c('cpc', 'mcphadenzhang2002', 'mcphadenzhang2004')}. These describe the background; their contribution to this event is not quantified (grade C).</p>
<p><b>Indian Ocean.</b> Cool SST appeared in the eastern Indian Ocean along and south of the equator from strong upwelling along the equator and Sumatra, and warm anomalies developed in the west from July 1997, peaking in February 1998 {c('murtugudde2000')}; the CPC dipole index rose from +0.55 °C in July to +1.39 °C in November {c('cpc')}. One analysis attributes the basin's SST changes largely to ENSO's effect on the atmosphere — the reversal of the Walker circulation and displaced south-east trades {c('yurienecker1999')}; two papers published together in 1999 described coupled ocean–atmosphere dynamics in the Indian Ocean during 1997–98 and a dipole mode of the tropical Indian Ocean (titles only; texts not read) {c('webster1999', 'saji1999')}. Its timing, after Pacific coupling, makes the IOD a consequence for onset; coupled forecast experiments suggest an extreme IOD can enhance El Niño {c('luo2010')}, but this was not tested here for 1997.</p>
<p><b>Tropical Atlantic.</b> The tropical North Atlantic was near or slightly below normal in early 1997 (−0.37 °C in March) and warm in 1998 (+0.51 °C in May) {c('cpc')}. Spring cooling in the north tropical Atlantic has been proposed as an El Niño trigger in general {c('ham2013')}; for 1997 this is untested (grade C/D), and the 1998 warming is the usual lagged response.</p>
<p><b>Volcanic and external forcing.</b> {d.get('volcano_sentence', 'A catalogue check for large tropical eruptions in 1995–97 is pending.')}</p>
<p><b>Background warming.</b> The tropical-mean adjustment in RONI changes the peak by only 0.09 °C (RONI +2.28, ONI +2.37) {c('lheureux2024', 'cpc')}. Contemporary debate concerned whether the post-1976 run of warm events was unusual {c('trenberthhoar1997')}; projections of more frequent extreme El Niños under greenhouse warming {c('cai2014')} do not attribute this event. No event-specific attribution is made here.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "synthesis", "title": "7. Causal synthesis and competing explanations", "html": f"""<p>The most plausible sequence is: (1) recharge after the 1995–96 La Niña created a deep warm-water reservoir by late 1996; (2) a run of strong, MJO-organised and extratropically amplified westerly wind events from December 1996 to June 1997 forced downwelling Kelvin waves and moved the warm pool's edge east; (3) the resulting central-Pacific warming in March–April and coastal warming from March merged by May–June, when the Bjerknes feedback took over; (4) the seasonal southward shift of the westerlies from November and basin discharge ended the event abruptly in May 1998.</p>
<p>The main competing view weights the slow ocean more heavily: with recharge and western-boundary reflection already favourable {c('picaut2002', 'delcroix2000')}, an El Niño was likely regardless of the bursts. The available quantitative answer splits the difference — roughly 40% initial state and 50% wind stress at 1 June {c('vanoldenborgh2000')} — but comes from one ocean model forced with one wind product. A noise-driven ensemble started at the end of 1996 favours a moderate event over the observed extreme {c('fedorov2003')}, which supports the view that the bursts set the amplitude rather than the occurrence. Bergman et al. reach a similar conclusion for the MJO: relevant to timing and initial growth rather than responsible for the event itself {c('bergman2001')}.</p>
<p>Unresolved: how much of the burst activity was itself a response to the eastward-extended warm pool; whether the Indian Ocean fed back on the peak; and the role of extratropical modes beyond the documented cold surges.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "knowable", "title": "8. What was forecast at the time", "html": f"""<p>This was the first very strong El Niño predicted in real time by many groups. Most models forecast some warming one to two seasons before the boreal-spring onset, but none predicted its strength until it was already becoming very strong in late spring; the best forecasts of Niño 3.4 before positive anomalies appeared were about +1 °C against an observed +2.5 to +3 °C {c('barnston1999')}. Against a climatology-and-persistence baseline, no model anticipated even half the peak amplitude at 6–11 months' lead, and none was skilful for both onset and decay at 6–8 months {c('landseaknaff2000')}. Once the event had developed, more models captured the late-1997 peak and the 1998 reversal {c('barnston1999')}. The rapid succession of bursts in early 1997 was a relatively rare event, which limits predictability {c('fedorov2003', 'vanoldenborgh2000')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "uncertainty", "title": "9. Uncertainties", "html": f"""<ul>
<li>The 40/50% decomposition comes from one ocean model and wind product {c('vanoldenborgh2000')}.</li>
<li>Several central studies (McPhaden 1999; Webster et al. 1999; Saji et al. 1999; Takayabu et al. 1999) were read only as bibliographic records or abstracts.</li>
<li>Reanalysis burst and MJO diagnostics depend on index and definition; RMM and OLR-based indices can disagree for the same event {c('kiladis2014')}.</li>
<li>Off-equatorial and sub-thermocline conditions before Argo are less certain than the TAO line itself.</li>
</ul>"""})

    S.append({"id": "conclusion", "title": "10. Conclusion", "html": f"""<p>The 1997–98 El Niño developed from a recharged Pacific, but its speed and size came from a rare run of westerly wind events in December 1996 – June 1997, organised by strong MJOs and intensified by twin cyclones tied to East Asian cold surges. Those events forced Kelvin waves and an eastward jump of the warm pool that joined central and eastern warming by May–June, after which coupled feedback carried the event to a December peak. Its demise, set in motion by the seasonal southward shift of the westerlies, was as abrupt as its onset. Compared with 1982–83, the trigger is far better documented: the ocean was observed directly, and a quantitative separation of precondition and wind forcing exists for this event.</p>"""})
    return S
